  "seoBody": "<div class=\"key-takeaways\"><strong>Key takeaways</strong><ul><li>A solar PV install is a notifiable electrical job under BS 7671 and Part P, and an MCS installation if it claims the Microgeneration Certification Scheme</li><li>Grid connection needs a DNO notification: G98 for small single-premises systems, G99 where G98 limits are exceeded</li><li>Commissioning captures per-string voltage and current, DC insulation resistance, earthing, inverter settings and AC checks, plus photos of the array, isolators and labelling</li><li>The handover pack pulls that together with the MCS certificate, the electrical certificate, the DNO paperwork and client guidance</li><li>Software captures the data and generates the report; it does not replace MCS certification, a competent installer or a calibrated test instrument</li></ul></div><h2>What solar PV commissioning software is actually for</h2><p>A grid-connected solar PV installation is an electrical installation. It falls under BS 7671, the 18th Edition wiring regulations, and the work is notifiable under Part P in England and Wales. If the install is claimed under the Microgeneration Certification Scheme, it must also meet the MCS installation standards and the IET Code of Practice for Grid-Connected Solar PV.</p><p>Software does not make any of that valid. The MCS-certified installer does, working to the standards with calibrated instruments. What software does is make the commissioning record easier to complete correctly on site, harder to forget a string test or a label, and faster to deliver as a clean handover pack. A good tool means the installer does not rebuild the commissioning data from memory in the van on the way home.</p><h2>Grid connection: G98, G99 and the DNO</h2><p>Before or shortly after energising, the Distribution Network Operator must be notified. Small single-premises systems within the limits generally use the G98 connect-and-notify route. Larger systems, or several installations at one site, fall under G99 with an application before connection. The engineering recommendations sit under the Energy Networks Association framework and the thresholds change over time. Quickler can capture the reference numbers, the inverter type-test details and photographs of the isolation and metering, as commissioning evidence. It is not the DNO portal. Check the current G98 and G99 thresholds and your DNO's process. This is guidance, not legal advice.</p><h2>The commissioning tests that go in the record</h2><h3>1. DC string tests</h3><p>Per string, record the open-circuit voltage and the short-circuit or operating current, and confirm they sit within the expected range for the modules and the irradiance on the day.</p><h3>2. Insulation, earthing and polarity</h3><p>Insulation resistance of the DC circuits, continuity of earthing and bonding, and correct polarity, recorded from a calibrated multifunction tester.</p><h3>3. Inverter and AC side</h3><p>Confirm the inverter is set to the correct grid code region, and complete the AC checks the electrical installation certificate covers.</p><h3>4. Isolators, labelling and photos</h3><p>DC and AC isolators, labelling and signage in place, photographed at the point of test rather than sorted out later.</p><h2>From commissioning data to the handover pack</h2><p>The homeowner should leave with a handover pack: the MCS certificate, the electrical installation certificate, the commissioning results, the DNO notification reference, the array and inverter details, the warranties and running guidance. RECC and HIES set consumer protection expectations for firms selling to consumers, and a clean pack is part of meeting them. Quickler pulls the commissioning data and photos into one report the moment the job is signed off. It complements the MCS certificate and the electrical certificate; it does not replace either.</p><h2>What to look for in commissioning software</h2><ul><li><strong>On-site completion:</strong> the tool should make it easier to record tests during commissioning, not afterwards</li><li><strong>Photo evidence at the point of test:</strong> array, isolators, inverter and labelling tied to a timestamp, not a folder sorted out that evening</li><li><strong>Clean handover output:</strong> a pack the homeowner, MCS auditor and insurer accept without explanation</li><li><strong>Only pay for who works:</strong> charging for every seat punishes firms for office staff and subcontractors. Quickler charges 20 pounds per active user per month, dormant users free, unlimited users</li><li><strong>Evidence trail:</strong> timestamp, installer, address and every reading, for the MCS audit or dispute that surfaces years later</li></ul><div class=\"callout\"><strong>Honest note:</strong> Quickler captures the commissioning data and generates the handover report. It does not make an install MCS compliant, it does not submit the DNO notification, and it is not a measuring instrument. It records the values the installer reads off a calibrated tester. The MCS-certified installer and the calibrated instrument carry the validity. <a href=\"/pages/help/index.html#contact-form-section\">Ask us</a> if that distinction matters for your firm.</div><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is a solar PV commissioning report?</h3><p>It is the record made when a photovoltaic system is energised and tested: per-string open-circuit voltage and current, DC insulation resistance, earthing and polarity, inverter grid settings, AC checks, and photos of the array, isolators and labelling. It forms part of the handover pack alongside the MCS certificate, the electrical installation certificate and the DNO notification.</p><h3>Does a solar PV install have to follow MCS?</h3><p>MCS is not compulsory for every install, but it is required if the system is to be certified under the Microgeneration Certification Scheme, which most homeowners and scheme routes expect. An MCS install must meet the MCS installation standards and the IET Code of Practice for Grid-Connected Solar PV, and the electrical work must comply with BS 7671. Check the current MCS scheme requirements, as they change.</p><h3>Do I need to notify the DNO for a solar PV system?</h3><p>Yes. Grid-connected PV requires notification to the Distribution Network Operator. Small single-premises systems generally use the G98 connect-and-notify route, while larger systems or multiple installations at one site fall under G99 with an application before connection. Confirm the current G98 and G99 thresholds and your DNO's process, as the engineering recommendations change over time.</p><h3>Can I capture PV commissioning over WhatsApp?</h3><p>Yes. Quickler's commissioning workflow runs over the WhatsApp Business API. The installer receives each question in their existing WhatsApp chat, replies with the test reading, a voice note or a photo, and the handover report generates automatically. No separate app or login is required, and Quickler manages the WhatsApp Business API account on the firm's behalf. It records the values the installer reads off a calibrated tester; it is not the tester.</p><h3>Related guides</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/pages/ev-charger-installation-report-uk.html\">EV charger installation report</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/heat-pump-commissioning-checklist-uk.html\">Heat pump commissioning checklist</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/battery-storage-inspection-report-uk.html\">Battery storage inspection report</a></li><li><a href=\"/pages/eicr-software-uk.html\">EICR and electrical reporting software</a></li></ul>",
